{
  "title": "Redesign the Estimated calories section so it degrades gracefully instead of listing every unmatched ingredient",
  "description": "The calorie estimate section (web: ramekin-ui/src/components/CalorieEstimate.tsx, iOS: ramekin-ios/Ramekin/CalorieEstimateSection.swift) renders the server's prose summary plus one bullet per unknown ingredient with its raw reason (\"kosher salt: No supported nutrition match\"). \
Against the prod corpus (994 recipes, audited 2026-09-27) the matcher currently resolves 7.6% of ingredient lines and 0 recipes get a complete estimate, so every recipe shows a wall of failure bullets and a meaningless 'partial subtotal' number. \
Even after the matcher improves (see the p1-ingredient-catalog-step* series), some recipes will stay partial, so the UI needs a coherent design for incomplete data.\n\
\n\
Recommended UX:\n\
1. \
Lead with one number, not prose: per serving when servings is known (e.g. '~520 kcal / serving'), else whole-recipe. \
The server should return structured fields (range, coverage) and stop shipping presentation sentences (summary / per_serving_summary) for clients to render verbatim.\n\
2. \
Gate on unknown lines, not on a calorie share: unknown lines have no calorie value, so their share of the total can't be computed. \
The server classifies every line as matched / negligible (per-line rule from the catalog step 1 issue: zero_calorie foods like salt and water, or trace_ok foods like pepper and spices with no quantity or a trace unit; spices with a real quantity are computed, not dropped) / not-a-food (headers) / unknown, and returns the count and names of unknown lines. \
Zero unknown lines: show the number. \
One or two unknown lines: show the number explicitly as a lower bound ('at least ~520 kcal / serving, excluding X'). \
More than that: don't show a number; show 'Not enough ingredient data to estimate calories' with the disclosure available. \
Pick the cutoff from the audit; it's a count of unknown lines, which the estimator can compute.\n\
3. \
Put the per-ingredient breakdown behind a disclosure ('How is this calculated?'), listing every line with its kcal contribution or a short human label ('not recognized', 'amount unclear'), never internal reason strings. \
This is also where a future 'fix this match' affordance would live.\n\
4. \
Exclude from the unknown count only lines positively classified as not-a-food (section headers) or negligible under that per-line rule (e.g. 'salt and pepper to taste'). \
A real food without a usable quantity ('oil for frying', 'butter for greasing') stays unknown, so it can never make a partial subtotal look complete.\n\
5. \
Keep the USDA attribution line in the disclosure, not the main view.\n\
\n\
Both web and iOS need the change. \
Coverage classification and thresholds must live server-side (ramekin-core nutrition::estimate) so clients only render; per doc/client-logic-sharing.md, don't duplicate the threshold logic in both clients. \
The API response shape changes, so regenerate clients and update tests/test_*.py end-to-end coverage plus CalorieEstimateViewModelTests.\n\
\n\
Re-run the audit (dump current_version ingredients from the prod DB and replay nutrition::estimate) to pick the threshold; the goal is that most recipes show either a trustworthy number or a single clean 'not enough data' line.",
  "status": "open",
